{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785236491,"nonce":"phnUjUOWaBmioeuY+LoLLa/7c7E=","title":"scarab-storage: the forward mtime-to-unix-ms conversion now has three private copies","message":"`scarab_storage::system_time_from_unix_ms` is public and is the domain crate's single statement of the **reverse** conversion (unix ms to `SystemTime`). `farm.rs` imports it rather than re-deriving it, and its doc comment says why: a second copy is a second thing to keep in step by hand.\n\nThe **forward** direction has no such public home, so it has been written privately three times — in `scarab-storage-s3`, in `scarab-workspace-client`, and now in `scarab-server/src/settle.rs`.\n\nThis is the fidelity contract's own arithmetic (ADR-0061 s7 pinned mode/mtime fidelity after measuring that losing it silently degraded cross-Step incremental compilation). Three private copies of a conversion that decides a snapshot **root** — a root moves when an mtime moves — is three chances for one of them to round differently and produce a different address for identical bytes.\n\n- [ ] Add the forward conversion to `scarab-storage` beside `system_time_from_unix_ms`\n- [ ] Replace all three private copies\n- [ ] One test pinning round-trip behaviour at a sub-millisecond boundary, since truncation-vs-rounding is exactly where two copies would diverge","files":null}]}